# Metacircular interpreter

A **metacircular interpreter** is an interpreter for a programming language
written in that same language. It is the central example of reflective
computation, in which programs treat programs as data. Unlike a [[quine]],
which only reproduces a description of itself, a reflective interpreter can
reason about a description: read it, run it, and change how it is run.

## Description

### Programs as data

Reflective computation is class F of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]]. Its
mechanism is that a program is represented as a value the language can
handle. Once that is so, an interpreter is an ordinary function from program
values to results, and it can be written in the language it interprets. The
classic case is the Lisp evaluator defined in Lisp. A [[self-hosting-compiler]]
is the same idea for compilation: the compiler for a language is a program in
that language, and compiles its own source.

The step that matters is the first one, the representation. It is the
computational counterpart of [[godel-numbering]], which represents formulas as
numbers so that arithmetic can handle them.

### Two levels, and what connects them

A metacircular interpreter has an [[object-and-meta-level]] structure in plain
view. The interpreted program is at the object level. The interpreter is at the
meta level. Because both are written in one language, anything at the meta
level can be handed down as data, and anything at the object level can be
handed up to be run. Whether that connection produces a [[strange-loop]]
depends on what is done with it.

- If the interpreter merely runs programs, there are two levels and no loop.
- If the interpreted program can inspect and alter the interpreter that is
  running it, the levels are crossed and the result returns to where it
  started. That is a candidate loop.

The discussion states the limit in the same sentence as for quines: neither
self-reproduction nor reflective interpretation implies incompleteness.
Reflection gives a system access to itself. A theorem about its limits needs a
further step, [[diagonalization]].

### External execution and internal representation

When the discussion proposed rebuilding Gödel's construction as a program, with
syntax trees, an encoding, substitution and proof checking, it insisted on one
distinction: executing the construction externally is not the same as proving
that the theory represents the construction internally. A metacircular
interpreter is on the first side. It executes. That the language can express
its own evaluator is a fact about programs. That a theory can prove things
about its own proofs is a fact about a [[reflective-formal-system]]. The first
is an analogy for the second and not an instance of it.

### Why it matters for this vault

A vault of cards with executable cells is a small reflective system. A cell
retrieves a card as a value, and the card may be the one that holds the cell.
That is programs as data, with documents in the role of programs. The
[[observer-card-experiment]] is the smallest design that uses it. The essay on
AI-oriented programming asks for the same property from a language: an agent
should be able to reason about a program, run it, inspect the result, and
revise its own description of the problem.

## Relations

- It is a kind of [[self-reference]] {relation: is-a}, by interpretation.
- It belongs to class F of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}.
- It contrasts with the [[quine]] {relation: contrasts}: reasoning about a description against copying one.
- It makes the [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses} distinction concrete, with both levels in one language.
- It is the computational counterpart of [[godel-numbering]] {relation: analogous-to}: programs as data, formulas as numbers.
- It is a different thing from a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: distinguished-from}: executing a construction against representing it inside a theory.
- It is not automatically a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}; that needs the interpreted program to act on its interpreter.
- It is the mechanism a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: generalizes} builds on.
- It is the principle behind the [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: example-of}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[quine]].** A program that prints its own source. It has access to its
  own text and only emits it, which is the weaker of the two abilities.
- **[[object-and-meta-level]].** The distinction between what is described and
  the description. In an interpreter the two are the running program and the
  program that runs it.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A theory with an encoding of its own syntax
  and proof relations. It is reflection established by proof inside the
  system, not by running something outside it.
- **[[observer-card-experiment]].** A card whose cell retrieves the card
  itself at a pinned revision. It is the document version of an interpreter
  examining its own source.

